  "query_preview": "..\n\nThrough the Weekend...\n\nAs we remain between the low to our NW and the high to our SE, \nthis southwesterly flow will bring several shortwaves through the \narea bringing repeated chances for showers and storms. The first \nof which is impacting us now with scattered showers and storms \nacross the region. None of the showers and storms will be \nefficient rainfall producers due to the lack of moisture. PWATs \ntoday are only sitting around 0.6\" for most of the CWA. Another\nshot of showers is possible tomorrow, however it looks like the \ngreatest chances will be to our NW closer to the main upper level \ncirculation. In terms of severe, any storm on Wednesday is capable\nof producing strong wind gusts associated with downburst activity\nbut most of the stronger storms will be north and west of our \nforecast area. \n\nStarting tomorrow there may be a notable difference between the \nhighs in our eastern plains versus the foothills in the west as \nthe two aforementioned regimes jockey for dominance. Places like \nBaker and Miles City may see highs 5 to 10F above average into \nthe 90s while Livingston keeps temps a touch below average in the \n70s. Winds will be a little breezy Wednesday and Thursday with the\nadvancing shortwaves but models have remained on the lower side of\nthings over the past day or so. Widespread gusts in the 20s mph \nare forecast both days with slightly higher (30s mph with isolated\nup to 40 mph) in the gap areas of the south-centeral Montana \nfoothills. These conditions combined with Min RH values in the \nteens to low 20s will result in elevated fire weather concerns \nWednesday and critical conditions Thursday, esepcailly low \nelevations west of Billings. Locations in the higher terrain \nrecently received 0.5-1\" of precip and thus fire concerns decrease\nnotably with elevation.\n\nThis weekend still has a lot of uncertainty around it with when \nand where exactly we will shortwaves move through our area. Most \nmodel runs agree that the main low will stay over the PNW through \na majority of the weekend. There will still likely be lobes of \nenergy that eject off of the parent feature and move into MT \nthrough the weekend, however just how much precip this brings and\nhow far east remains a large question. It does look like easterly\nlow level flow will return Saturday which will help increase \nmoisture in the low levels and, in turn, theta-e values. This \nwould lead to better chances for thunderstorms if a lifting \nmechanism is present. WMR",
